course: dictionary
lesson: 4440
title: Handrail: a word for safety
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: A handrail is a long bar you hold on to for support and safety. The clues are in the words around it: «Holding the handrail lets you catch yourself if you trip or slip.» Trip and slip are ways you can fall, so a handrail must be something that stops you falling.
explain: Look at how the word is built: hand plus rail. A rail is a long bar, and a handrail is the bar your hand grips. Ask yourself: what word would fit here? «Hold the handrail, take one step at a time» only makes sense if the handrail is something you grip, so the meaning fits.
explain: Handrails are not only on stairs. Ramps, bridges and baths can have them too. When you are not sure, swap your meaning into the sentence and check that it still makes sense.
fact: A handrail is a long bar you hold on to for support and safety.
fact: «Handrail» is a noun, and its plural is «handrails».
fact: Handrails can be found along stairs, ramps, bridges and baths.
fact: On an escalator, the handrail moves at the same speed as the steps.
fact: Phrases to remember: «hold the handrail», «use the handrail» and «stair handrail».
exercise: «Holding the handrail lets you catch yourself if you trip or slip.» What is a handrail for? | *Staying steady and safe when you move | Pulling the steps up for you | Making the stairs look pretty
exercise: «On an escalator, stand still, hold the handrail, keep feet in the middle.» What should you do with the handrail on an escalator? | Sit on it to rest | Run along it to the top | *Hold it while you stand still | Slide down it
exercise: «Hold the handrail, take one step at a time and put your whole foot on each step.» Find and copy the word that names the bar you grip. | whole | time | *handrail | step
test: Where is a handrail usually found? | Up in the clouds | Inside a bag | *Along stairs and bridges | Under the sea
test: Which is NOT safe on an escalator? | *Sliding down the handrail | Stepping off at the top | Standing still with feet in the middle | Holding the handrail
exam: Why does holding the handrail help you on the stairs? | It keeps your shoes clean | It means you never need to walk | It makes the stairs go faster | *It lets you catch yourself if you trip or slip
